            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Domestic Policy and the \"Jamahiriya\" System</strong><br>\nGaddafi introduced the \"Jamahiriya,\" or \"state of the masses,\" in Libya, which he claimed was a form of direct democracy, though in practice it was considered authoritarian, with power concentrated in his hands.</p>\n\n<p><strong>c1</strong><br>\nEarly Life and Graffiti Art</p>\n\n<p><strong>Jean-Michel Basquiat was born in Brooklyn, New York, in 1960. He started as a graffiti artist in the late 1970s under the tag SAMO, gaining notoriety for his enigmatic epigrams on the lower Manhattan landscape. His street-based work played a significant role in his rise to fame within the art community.</strong><br>\nTransition to Fine Art</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
